    id 442
    label "2XF2qgsq5iQ"
    step 0
    date "2022-07-13T19:14:25Z"
    title "Flooding destroys lives in Pakistan! The horrors of climate change will affect everyone!"
    description "Flooding in Pakistan has claimed the lives of dozens of people due to monsoon rains that hit the country.&#10;&#10;Officials said severe flooding in Pakistan has killed dozens and left hundreds homeless as torrential monsoon rains hit the country.&#10;&#10;In the southern province of Balochistan, 57 people, including women and children, have died after being swept away by rising waters, Ziaullah Langove, advisor to the provincial chief minister for emergencies and home affairs, said on Saturday. According to Langov, eight dams burst due to heavy rains.&#10;&#10;Hundreds more people were left homeless after their homes collapsed in the rain and flood waters, he said, adding that torrential monsoon rains continued.&#10;&#10;In the northwestern Pakistani province of Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, two people, including a six-year-old child, were killed and four injured when their home collapsed, according to an official statement from the district.&#10;&#10;Heavy rains have hit Pakistan in recent days, causing large swaths of Karachi's largest city to be flooded. The Pakistan Navy said it was joining forces to evacuate civilians and bring food and fresh water to Balochistan. In 2010, the worst floods in memory affected 20 million people in Pakistan, damage to infrastructure ran into the billions of dollars, and vast amounts of crops were destroyed in one go. A fifth of the country was flooded.&#10;&#10;The seasonal monsoon rains, a lifeline for farmers across South Asia, also typically cause loss of life and property damage each year. According to the Ministry of Environment and Climate Change, Pakistan received 87 percent more rain this season than in previous years.&#10;&#10;In neighboring Afghanistan, 24 people have died as a result of floods in the east and south of the country, a spokesman for the disaster management agency said. Last month's monsoon rains also triggered widespread flooding in northeast Bangladesh and India, displacing about six million people and killing dozens. Flooding in Bangladesh has been described by a government expert as potentially the worst in the country since 2004.&#10;&#10;In India and Bangladesh, extreme weather events have also increased in recent years, causing large-scale damage.&#10;&#10;Environmentalists warn that climate change could lead to more natural disasters, especially in low-lying and densely populated areas of Bangladesh.&#10;&#10;Subscribe to the channel so that as many people as possible open their eyes to the problems of irreversible climate change and the planet! Be among us! Together we can pay attention to the problems of the earth!"
